---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getSiteSettings } from '../../lib/sanity.js';

const settings = await getSiteSettings();
---
<Base
  title="About | hredd.org"
  description="Why hredd.org exists, its editorial position, and how to contribute."
  activePage="about"
>
  <section class="prose-page page-pad">
    <h1>About hredd<span style="color: var(--accent);">&middot;</span>org</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      Most writing about human rights and environmental due diligence is
      produced in the jurisdictions that write the laws. This publication is
      produced in the jurisdictions that answer to them.
    </p>

    <h2>The mission</h2>
    <p>
      hredd.org tracks every mandatory HREDD regime that reaches Global South
      supply chains, and analyses what those regimes mean for the workers,
      farmers, contractors and enterprises who experience them as
      questionnaires, audits and contract clauses rather than as legislation.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tracker is maintained monthly, every entry carries a changelog, and
      every claim links to a primary source. Analysis is written for
      practitioners: sustainability managers at mills and factories, programme
      staff at civil society organisations, and anyone who has ever had to
      translate a directive into a field protocol.
    </p>

    <h2>The editorial position</h2>
    <p>
      This publication supports mandatory due diligence and is impatient with
      how it is currently done. Those two positions are compatible, and holding
      both at once is the entire point. Disclosure without consequences has
      demonstrably failed, yet compliance regimes designed without supplier
      knowledge produce burden without protection, and both failures deserve
      documentation.
    </p>
    <p>
      hredd.org is independent, carries no advertising, and accepts no funding
      from companies within the scope of the laws it tracks.
    </p>

    <h2>The editor</h2>
    <p>
      Vir Mehta is a decent work and supply chain sustainability practitioner
      based in Noida, India. He has spent his career on the supplier side of
      agricultural and garment supply chains across South and Southeast Asia,
      authored the first practitioner-facing CSDDD self-assessment toolkit, and
      writes here in a personal capacity.
    </p>

    <h2>Contribute</h2>
    <p>
      Field reports, analysis and corrections are welcome, particularly from
      practitioners writing about laws as they land in producing countries.
      Pitches should be short, specific and grounded in first-hand knowledge of
      a supply chain.
    </p>

    <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Write to <a href={`mailto:${settings.contactEmail}`} style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);">{settings.contactEmail}</a>
      for pitches, corrections, tracker suggestions and anything else. Every
      message is read, though replies can take a week during field travel.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
